The role of environmental statisticians in environmental
policy: the case of performance measurement
Tanja Srebotnjak *
Yale School of Forestry and Environmental Studies, Yale University, 205 Prospect Street, New Haven, CT 06511, USA
1. Introduction
‘‘What gets measured gets done.’’ ‘‘If you cannot measure it, you cannot manage it.’’ Generally associated with situations of economic or corporate interest, phrases like these are nowadays increasingly heard in the environmental policy field. Although the driving forces of the ongoing ‘‘measurization’’ of environmental policy are manifold, including resource constraints, promotion of accountability, and reducing scientific uncertainty, the general trend towards greater use of and reliance on factual data – as well as statisticians and others trained to analyze them – is arguably a positive one.
1.1. The issues
Measuring important environmental policy objectives – from climate change to biodiversity, water quality, and ecosystem degradation due to air pollution – tends to be a difficult task. What are the main issues that hamper the use of quantitative data and scientists in the environmental policy process while other disciplines such as economics and the health sciences apply them so effectively to their advantage? For one there are the twin factors of complexity and perception. Environmental science can be overwhelmingly complex and uncertain. But while this true for economics and
