l statisticians have on the framing of policy questions, selection of appropriate study design, data collection and analysis, and the ultimate interpretation of the findings. In light of the growing environmental challenges such as global climate change, it is therefore timely to investigate the role of environmental statisticians in the creation of environmental information and policies and to identify strategies statisticians can bring to bear on bridging the data-policy gap.
1.2. Scope and structure of the paper
This paper focuses on the role of statisticians working at the nexus of environmental statistics and policy within the context of measuring environmental performance. It reviews experiences that an interdisciplinary team at the universities Yale and Columbia has made in measuring country-level environmental performance and translating it into policy messages. Specifically, it examines the recently published Pilot 2006 Environmental Performance Index (EPI), which is aimed to be a fact-based, statistically sound policy tool that helps countries achieve environmental objectives by tracking progress, identifying environmental ‘‘best practices’’, and providing strategic peer-group analyses. The EPI is therefore a suitable case study for the demand on environmental statisticians to create measures that balance methodological
transparency and the delivery of clear policy messages to the environmental community, on the one hand, with the use of appropriate statistical techniques and dealing with many sources of uncertainty on the other (Esty et al., 2006). The paper is organized as follows. Section 2 begins with an examination of the difficulties to define environmental performance in measurable terms and proposes to transfer relevant concepts and methodologies from economics and other social sciences. Section 3 focuses on environmental policy and its information needs. Since environmental data remain scarce both in quantity and quality, it is useful to focus for a moment on the particularly challenging problems of developing countries to produce and apply reliable statistical data for policymaking. Despite many persisting problems, the trend towards assessing environmental policy through performance measures continues unabated. Section 4 reviews the Pilot 2006 Environmental Performance Index (Esty et al., 2006) as a potential model for both developed and developing economies interested in tracking progress on environmental objectives. In this context, the need to create more policy-relevant indicators while coping with data gaps and uncertainties is considered as one of the areas of special importance. The paper concludes with an assessment of the reciprocal relationship between environmental policymakers and statisticians and how it could be strengthened.
2. Definitions and measurement
2.1. Problems defining environmental performance
Many different definitions of environmental performance can be found (Commission for Environmental Cooperation, 2000; Global Environmental Management Initiative, 1998; Schulze, 1999; Segnestam, 1999; United Nations Economic Commission for Europe, 2006). General agreement exists that performance refers to the assessment of actual (measured) results of a system against policy, objectives and targets (International Organization for Standardization, 2004). Variations exist with respect to the definition of the system (e.g., country, corporation, household), including its inherent characteristics, boundaries (e.g., closed or open to energy and material flows), and inclusion of effects from exchanges with other systems (e.g., trade and transboundary movement of people, pollution, and capital). For example, while some country-level performance assessments consider only the achievement of domestic environmental objectives, others request that transboundary effects such as long-range air pollution and resource exchanges through trade be taken into account. The accomplishment of stated objectives or targets in environmental performance measurement refers to the effectiveness of environmental policies. In addition, economic performance assessments evaluate the efficiency with which the objectives or targets have been attained. This is routinely done within the economic theory of maximizing net social welfare, which expresses costs and benefits in monetary terms. Transfer of this approach to environmental issues suffers from a number of limitations, including the lack of
