2.2. How does environmental performance measurement differ from economic and social performance assessments?
The previous section already touched upon the notion of efficiency as an important criterion in performance assessments in economics. Economists estimate efficiency in monetary units to make different policy choices comparable. Although progress is being made2, efficiency is not yet a standard component of environmental performance assessments. Environmental economists have applied, adapted, and expanded the theory of efficiency to the environmental field. The relatively new disciplines of environmental and natural resource economics have made substantial contributions to the development of new measurement methods, which in turn inspire more efficient environmental policies. Yet, methodological problems for estimating the costs and benefits to society of the impacts of the environmental policy and the large amounts of data necessary to conduct such estimations still constitute significant obstacles to applying efficiency criteria to environmental policy. Many other approaches to performance assessment can be found in the social sciences. For example, standardized
educational aptitude tests, health attainment scores, psychological evaluation protocols for sensitive occupations, and civil liberties indicators are built on extensive and growing bodies of research on the statistical modeling of multi-dimensional concepts. Indicators serving as representations or proxies for latent concepts such as intelligence, and other behavioral traits are ubiquitous and have gained widespread albeit not undisputed acceptance. The exploration and aggregation of environmental and related socio-economic data in the form of indicators and indices has experienced an unprecedented level of attention since the first United Nations Conference und the Human Environment in Stockholm in 1972. And since the majority of environmental policy concerns including climate change, biodiversity loss, land degradation, and water resource management are also multi-dimensional, their description and measurement could benefit from the testing and adaptation of measurement instruments developed in the social sciences at large. Are there other aspects that models in environmental and the social sciences have in common and can we learn from these linkages? Without laying claim to comprehensiveness and detail, there are a few similarities, which may have potential for the cross-disciplinary transfer of methods. For one, the examples of multi-dimensional concepts cited above also share their intrinsic complexity to measure them. Intelligence tests, for example, vary in their operationalization of the concept of intelligence. Useful insights could emerge from an examination of their design and the historical processes through which these tests have evolved into accepted measurement tools. Another common element of the environmental and social sciences is that the systems and processes are often not amenable to experimental control and replication. For example, many studies in both fields rely on ex-post observational data and cannot be replicated. Endogenous factors creating ‘noise’ in the system under investigation cannot easily be reduced and limit comparability across systems. Small sample sizes further hinder the identification of significant differences in the data. Statisticians have developed a rich body of theory and applications relevant to these and other issues. Examples thereof that are particularly useful in the context of analyzing environmental data for decisionmaking are methods for imputation and estimation of incomplete data (Dempster et al., 1977; Hartley and Hocking, 1971; Ibrahim et al., 2005; Little and Rubin, 2002; Rubin, 1996; Schafer and Graham, 2002), variance–covariance estimation in spatial-temporal models (as discussed by Guttorp, 2000), and techniques to optimize sampling designs, increase statistical power, and support economic evaluations of alternative sampling schemes (Benedetti-Cecchi, 2001; Fox, 2001; Smeach and Jernigan, 1977). Lastly, a common characteristic of performance measurement in the environmental and social science fields is the occurrence of processes over a broad range of time periods. Many ecological processes take decades, centuries or longer to complete while others occur within days or less. Similarly, behavioral studies record events over decades or hours. In both the possibility of spurious correlations or loss of ‘signal’ due to noise is large. How other disciplines eliminate or reduce such problems could offer solutions to the environmental field.
