3.1. The search for good data and ugly facts
It is not newsworthy to lament the continued lack of environmental and related socio-economic data. Although dedicated scientists, field observers, and technicians constantly fill gaps in the environmental knowledge puzzle, this lack remains a serious problem, especially with respect to the measurement of cause-effect and dose–response relationships as well as performance measurement. Often, the necessary data are not available because they cannot be measured directly, are too expensive to measure, information is not shared for reason of security interests, or the necessary data have not been collected due to faults in the planning process or scientific unknowns at the time of measurement. Other factors that pose additional burdens on the work of statisticians include measurement error, incompatibility of monitoring data across jurisdictions, inconsistent laboratory analysis techniques, a general lack of policy-relevant indicators, but also a frequently encountered resistance or negligence to incorporate statisticians’ input early on in study design and data collection processes. Environmental statisticians, therefore, often experience the difficult task to provide clear results for policy objectives on the basis of complex science, limited data, and many sources of uncertainty. The problem is far from new: Arnold Zellner in a 1992 editorial in JASA calls on statisticians to renew their attention to ‘‘get the facts’’ in the form of reliable data (Zellner, 1992). This should not be equated with a call to eradicate all sources of errors in the data (it would be prohibitively expensive and infeasible) but that such errors are acknowledged in the subsequent analysis, at least in the discussion of the findings but preferably through estimates of standard errors and confidence intervals. Furthermore, statistical data exploration and analysis comprises not only the search for neat patterns consistent with the investigators’ personal opinions or even with society’s broadly shared beliefs. Quoting Zellner (1992), environmental statisticians should strive to produce ‘‘ugly facts’’ and find explanations for them. After all, a new index of environmental performance that only confirms existing knowledge does not provide any new or additional value. Zellner advocates that we test existing theory, hypotheses, and beliefs more rigorously and develop new explanations for them if we find patterns inconsistent with our existing knowledge. Because ugly facts are easily dismissed as outliers, measurement errors, or other nuisances, they are often eliminated from the analysis. Distinguishing ‘‘good’’ from ‘‘bad’’ data is an exploratory process that requires the close interaction of subject-area experts, statisticians, and policymakers. In fact, path-breaking new insights into environmental processes have come about through the collection of good data and their analysis in interdisciplinary teams. An example is the discovery of the ozone hole over the Antarctic by British scientists Farman, Gardiner, and Shanklin (The Ozone Hole Inc., 2006). The drop in ozone concentrations in the 1980s was initially attributed to faulty measurement instruments. Instead, thorough (statistical) analysis revealed a seasonal and widening area of thinning stratospheric ozone concentrations, which prompted a rare
case of global environmental co-operation to gradually phaseout the production of six major CFCs3. As the above example demonstrates, environmental policy perhaps more than other policy areas – is concerned not with average but with extreme events. This requires specialized analytical tools and statisticians are pre-disposed to contribute to their development. Zidek (2006), for instance, promotes the term post-normal statistical science (PNS) for the new era of increasing intertwining of environmental statistics and policy. PNS refers to the complexity of environmental policy questions, the high level of data and scientific uncertainty surrounding them, and the presence of a plurality of divergent societal values and policy perspectives. As he puts it succinctly, it ‘‘. . . embraces issue-driven scientific inquiry related to environmental controversies; the facts are uncertain, values in dispute, stakes high, and decisions urgent’’ (Zidek, 2006). Environmental performance measurement exhibits the typical symptoms of PNS and statisticians are, therefore, needed even more urgently to contribute their knowledge of analyzing extreme values, dealing with uncertainty, and testing model sensitivity to the decisionmaking process that is needed to arrive at meaningful, consensual policies. Those working on the design of environmental indicators for performance or sustainability are indeed breaking new ground in ‘‘mandated science’’, i.e., science at the intersection of values, policy, and traditional, independent core science (Zidek, 2006). Mandated science inevitably requires statisticians to balance scientific impartiality with the scope and purpose of the research they are carrying out. Frameworks for environmental performance assessment, for example, are influenced by the worldviews of those developing them and/or possibly those funding their development. Emphasizing the collection of good data and an appreciation of unusual, ugly facts might help increase the credibility of environmental performance assessments. This is especially needed given the continued interest but growing skepticism directed at their results. The latter is a direct consequence of the growing number of indicator sets for measuring sustainability at large that Pinter et al. (2005) call the ‘‘indicator zoo’’ and that are developed from limited information, different frameworks, and whose interpretation subsequently generates mixed messages about environmental conditions and likely future trends (Wilson et al., 2007).
3.2. Environmental statistics and environmental policy from the perspective of developing countries
Chapter 40 of Agenda 21 (United Nations, 1992) put the need for environmental information center stage in the international environmental and development theater. In particular, world leaders at the 1992 United Nations Conference on Environment and Development (UNCED) in Rio de Janeiro, Brazil, acknowledged that the gap in data availability, quality, and accessibility is widening between developed and developing countries. There is a growing body of research into the
