drivers and implications of the knowledge divide between the global North and South (cf. Karlsson, 2002 for an overview on this subject). To counter the information gap, a number of international action plans were developed, data collection programs implemented, and environmental ministries and agencies created but 15 years later, the gap is not closing. Environmental performance measurement today is thus confronted with a seeming abundance of data for industrialized or developed countries (seeming because the available data are not always the most needed) and a substantial information scarcity for the rest of the world4. In the following, I therefore review specifically the situation of environmental statistics in the developing world. In the aftermath of the Stockholm and Rio conferences, a general ‘‘stock-taking’’ of available environmental information took place in many countries; often under the leadership of the national statistical office. Both OECD and developing nations identified the existence of considerable cross-sectoral dispersion of environmental information. This fragmentation of data collection efforts across line ministries and governmental agencies (agriculture, forestry and fisheries, health, planning and development, tourism, etc.) is both helpful and problematic as it means that a variety of data pertaining to environmental issues already existed but was generally not collected specifically for environmental policy purposes. Indeed, much information to this day is stored in incompatible formats and databases, access-restricted, and organized according to incompatible classifications or definitions. An example of the dispersion of environmental information is the measurement of pollution as one of the first environmental policy objectives (Polfeldt, 2006). Yet, data on pollutant emissions, their ambient concentrations, and ultimately their health effects are scattered across different governmental agencies, collected according to varying protocols, or missing. A frequently observed upstream problem of information dispersion is a general lack of coordination across sectors and ministries, which leads to the duplication of efforts and critical information gaps elsewhere. Nevertheless, the earlier and more successful growth of environmental movements in OECD countries, including the founding of ‘‘green’’ political parties, sparked consolidation efforts sooner and at a faster pace compared to developing countries5. Another factor that limited the effective production
and use of environmental information was the relative weakness of national statistical services in many developing states, particularly with respect to the necessary expertise in environmental data collection and analysis. Polfeldt (2006) points out that regardless of the many obstacles, at least 30 developing countries released State of the Environment (SoE) reports in the aftermath of the Rio Summit. Many of those, however, remained one-time efforts because the countries could or would not consider sustaining them in the long-term. International, regional, and bilateral capacity building projects over the past two decades have generated multiple benefits for environmental statistics as a new field of official statistics in developing countries. They helped integrate environmental concerns into national policy strategies; built legitimacy for newly created environmental ministries and environmental protection agencies, improved cross-sectoral communication channels, and resulted in the training of critically needed environmental statisticians6. More substantial environmental reports with direct links to policy agendas are now published, albeit data quantity and quality still lacks OECD standards. They still often contain more pressure data such as population size, industrial activity, and extent of trade, which are generally easier to find than impact and outcome figures such as pollutant concentrations in air and water. I believe it is fair to assume that environmental statisticians in developing countries face similar but also additional challenges compared to their colleagues in the OECD member states. In the latter, people often express environmental concerns as among their highest priorities for policy action and environmental polls regularly find a widespread appreciation of environmental values (e.g., the environmental poll conducted by the Yale Center for Environmental Law & Policy, 2005). This creates public pressure on political actors to invest resources on issues such as environmental monitoring, data collection and analysis systems, training, and general environmental infrastructure building. In contrast, mainstreaming environmental concerns into policymaking is an uphill battle for many developing nations struggling to meet basic human needs. The environmental indicators available in these countries are, therefore, often only those mandated and/or funded through international or bilateral training and assistance programs or are part of international sustainable development strategies such as the Millennium Development Goals (United Nations, 2000). It is noteworthy that these indicators have been developed with global comparability in mind, to prod countries into action, or to facilitate the direction and allocation of development aid rather than their utility for individual countries. It remains to be seen if such measurement efforts create sustained benefits to the countries as well as a sense of national ownership or if they place undue burden on already overloaded statistical services (Statistical Commission, 2002).
