Even with limited resources and data, environmental statisticians in developing countries need to be trained in environmental policy or paired with policymakers to better link available information with policy goals. In the following section I present our experiences in the development of the Pilot 2006 Environmental Performance Index as an illustration of the challenges and opportunities arising in linking environmental policymaking to data-driven performance measurement.
4. Lessons from the 2006 Pilot EPI
The Pilot 2006 Environmental Performance Index is produced by the Yale Center for Environmental Law and Policy and Columbia University’s Center for International Earth Science Information Network (CIESIN)7 as a tool to assist policymakers in tracking their environmental performance. The EPI is a global index in the sense that environmental performance is evaluated for all 133 countries included in the index using a uniform set of six widely established environmental policy objectives: environmental health, clean air, clean and sufficient water, protected biodiversity and habitat, productive natural resources, and sustainable energy. Furthermore, within each of the policy categories between two and five indicators track progress against fixed performance targets (summarized in Table 1). The choice of the policy categories is informed by international policy consensus (e.g., the United Nations Millennium Development Goals) as well as the developers’
belief that these are areas of environmental policy that every country should strive to make progress in. The EPI indicators were chosen to as much as possible measure on-the-ground conditions since outcome measures are the ultimate gauge of successful policy (Esty et al., 2006). Performance targets are specified for each indicator on the basis of international agreements, the scientific literature, and expert consultation to promote long-term desirable public health and ecosystem sustainability. Performance analysis becomes more meaningful when metrics can be compared at different levels of aggregation, across sectors, and against different reference units. This requires that the indicators are comparable, e.g., by measuring them on the same scale. For this reason, a two-step procedure converts the EPI indicators to proximity-to-target values, which reflect how closely current performance approaches the targets. First, for each indicator country values below the 5th percentile were set to the 5th percentile value to avoid outliers skewing the distribution and in the few cases where a country exceeded the target, its value was set to the target, thereby not rewarding ‘‘overperformance’’. In the second step, the indicators were transformed to a scale from 0 to 100 using a simple arithmetic procedure such that a final indicator score of 100 is equal to meeting the target and a value of 0 corresponds to the worst possible or worst observed value8. The EPI allows performance analysis at three levels of aggregation. At the first step, scores for the six policy categories are calculated as the weighted average of the corresponding proximity-to-target indicators. The weighting applied in this step is derived from both expert consultation
