4.1. Coping with data gaps, lack of quality, and relevance
Any performance assessment can only be as good as the data that feed into it. The three most pressing data shortcomings affecting the EPI are:
Lack of policy-relevant indicators (or the data for them). Incomplete data sets, i.e., with respect to temporal and/or spatial coverage. Varying levels of data quality, specifically as it relates to measurement error and cross-country comparability of data
vis-a` -vis underlying definitions, data collection methodology, and quality control protocols.
Since environmental statistics is a relatively new discipline (Guttorp, 2000), it means that time series of environmental indicators are often short or intermittent. The cost of environmental monitoring activities and their considerable technical requirements also contribute to the inadequacy of the quantity of available information especially in developing countries (cf. Section 3.2 on the disparities in environmental data availability in developed and developing countries). Harmonization efforts and data quality standards also have had a fairly short history in environmental statistics compared to other disciplines. Although regional and international organizations such as the Statistical Office of the European Communities (EUROSTAT) and the United Nations Statistics Division (United Nations, 2004) are actively promoting their development and implementation, comparability of environmental data continue to be very limited. Lastly and perhaps most importantly, despite investment and successes in building environmental information systems and databases (cf. Section 3.2), policy-relevant indicators are scarce. Among the reasons for the disconnect between data collection activities and policymaking are
Policy-specific indicators tend to be more complex, their scientific basis is more difficult, and the necessary information harder to collect. The channels of communication between subject-area experts, statisticians, and policymakers are insufficient and not reciprocal in the sense that policymakers seek statistical advice throughout all stages of the policy development process and statisticians actively see to contribute to apply statistical theory and applications to the policy process.
