4.2. Reconciling transparency and simplicity with statistical rigor
The use of statistical methods in index development to answer environmental science and policy questions is uneven across and within disciplines. Some applications worth mentioning are, inter alia, in ecology, the soil and water sciences, as well as in biodiversity protection. For example, based on a review of the statistical properties of widely used indices measuring species richness and abundance Buckland et al. (2005) develop a set of criteria an index should meet and show that a modified version of the Shannon index (Shannon and Weaver, 1949) has these properties. Chiu and Guttorp (2004a,b) build on the Benthic Index of Biotic Integrity (B-IBI) by Kerans and Karr (1994) and develop a stream health index with reduced bias, variability, and reduced sensitivity to changes in the metric scoring system used to construct the index. Soil scientists have developed indices for soil biota stress, droughts, and land management (Hortensius and Nortcliff, 1991; Orwin and Wardle, 2004; Smakhtin and Hughes, 2004; Syers et al., 1995). Washington (1984) reviews several performance indices pertaining to aquatic ecosystems. Other areas of environmental relevance, specifically sustainable development, and within its realm the theme of environmental sustainability and performance measurement, have produced a rapidly growing number of indicators and indices but while policy relevance was a primary focus, few of these efforts considered statistical properties such as bias, variance, and robustness to changes in the underlying assumptions explicitly in the design process (Parris and Kates, 2003; Pinter, 1994; Pinter et al., 2005; UNEP/RIVM, 1994; United Nations, 1991, 1994, 1996; United Nations Commission for Sustainable Development, 2001). The credibility of a performance measure hinges not the least on its methodological soundness. While the users of performance indices such as the EPI may not be interested in the finest detail of their calculation, transparency in the methods applied, the data transformations carried out, and the underlying model assumptions is essential for the index to gain credibility and acceptance. There might also exist opportunities for developers of environmental performance indices (and other aggregate environmental policy-relevant
measures) to adopt statistical methods from scientific indices in ecology, hydrology, etc. to address aspects such as metric selection, standardization, aggregation, and weighting. Often, however, transparency is at least partially equated with methodological simplicity, thereby calling for trade-offs between statistical sophistication and ease of understanding by non-technical audiences. The question thus arises whether the model should be built according to the best available knowledge or if it should prioritize simplicity and easy comprehension9? The answer is that the appropriate choice of statistical methods depends on the specific circumstances of the project. Indeed, a review of a more broadly defined set of measures used in environmental and development policy suggests that in practice, the solution lies somewhere in between10. Interestingly, very few of the papers and sources reviewed were (co-)authored by statisticians, which points to an opportunity for greater involvement of statistical experts in the development process. Notwithstanding the degree of statistical sophistication, existing performance measurement initiatives make use of a variety of statistical techniques with differing demands on the user’s technical knowledge. The EPI, for example, is constructed on the basis of expert opinions and statistical analyses (Esty et al., 2006). The choice of this middle-path is deliberate and reflects our explicit objective to create an easily comprehendible and usable policy tool, which is informed by and reliant on statistical techniques where appropriate and necessary to fulfill this goal. The development process was therefore designed to match broad user buy-in with statistical rigor. Nevertheless, the EPI is a work in progress, which includes the possibility for greater use of statistical tools once the available data have improved to the extent judged necessary by experts. Potential areas of statistical improvement include:
Build models for missing data imputation. Estimate confidence intervals for index scores taking into account measurement error. Test the usefulness of alternative weighting procedures such as Multi-Criteria-Analysis (MCA). Expand the scope of sensitivity and uncertainty analysis to include additional factors of sensitivity and uncertainty. Develop causal models for the driving forces of environmental performance and other policy interests.
At this point, our general experience suggests that practically useful environmental performance measures that resonate with diverse audiences should be transparent in the data and underlying assumptions and as methodologically simple as possible. Although Zellner’s ‘‘keep it sophisticatedly simple’’ variation (Zellner, 2007) on the well-known KISS
