4.3. Understanding the role of societal values in measuring environmental performance
The lack of consensus on how to measure environmental performance (and the broader concept of environmental sustainability) is reflected in the diversity of perspectives and societal values motivating or at least influencing existing definitions (Adriaanse, 1993; Azzone and Raffaella, 1994; Bandura, 2005; Fa ̈ re et al., 2004; Freudenberg, 2003; Grafton and Knowles, 2004; Hajer, 1992; Hammond et al., 1995; Organization for Economic Co-operation and Development, 1993, 1996; Segnestam, 1999; Tyteca, 1996; World Bank, 1996). As Levett (1998) in his discussion of indicators of quality of life points out ‘‘the search for better indicators is not only a technical problem. It is also . . . about . . . what it is that we value.’’ The role of personal and societal values in environmental policy is also of importance vis-a` -vis the concept of post-normal statistical science in that the indicator development process requires not only creative and technical expertise but also room for testing, critiquing, and finding consensus. Hence, measures of environmental performance developed at and for certain times and locales may not be found useful or even agreeable by other groups, in other times, and/or places. The Pilot EPI makes the influence of value judgments and need for further debate explicit (Esty et al., 2006):
‘‘Derived from a careful review of the environmental literature, these twin goals [environmental health and ecosystem vitality] mirror the priorities expressed by policymakers . . . The Pilot 2006 EPI represents a work in progress meant to stimulate debate on appropriate metrics and methodologies for tracking environmental performance, enable analysis of the determinants of environmental success, and highlight the need for increased investment in environmental indicators and data.’’
A number of conclusions can be drawn from the influence of value judgments on the development of measures of environmental performance:
Performance measures should be reviewed periodically to ensure they represent the goals and values of those they are intended to assist in decisionmaking and whose well-being is being affected by those decisions. There is no ‘‘right’’ measure or indicator set. Instead of searching for the universally accepted measure, focus should concentrate to make the measure ‘‘fit for purpose’’ (Levett, 1998). Composite measures, which involve weighting of the constituent components should be tested for the effect of the selected weights on the outcome and retain a degree of flexibility regarding the choice of the weights to accommodate different priorities. Such sensitivity analysis should extend to all subjective assumptions made in the design process.
4.4. Communicating results
Environmental performance assessments serve a diverse group of users, including scientists, technicians, policymakers, the media, and the general public. This puts different demands on the way the results are prepared and disseminated. Small, homogeneous groups such as scientific communities concerned, for example, with biodiversity protection, use their own technical jargon, graphical presentations, and other tools to effectively communicate with each other. In contrast, performance measures designed to appeal to policymakers and/or the non-trained face substantial diversity in backgrounds, access to communication channels (e.g., Internet, libraries, etc.), interests, and uses. Too little consideration is sometimes given to they way measurement results are presented and disseminated. Criteria commonly associated with a ‘good indicator’ often include a communication aspect (Chess et al., 2005). The challenge for the statistician is thus to combine methodological robustness with ease of comprehension. If the assessment is to promote public participation, the issue of competence should be considered at the outset: an indicator is not merely a representation of a phenomenon but also a communication tool in its own right. As Chess et al. (2005) summarizes: ‘‘indicators are meant to be representations of knowledge, selected through best available procedures, and easily interpreted. As such, they have the potential to promote ‘competence’ in public participation.’’ This goal is reflected in a distinct school of political thought. Advocates of deliberative democracy promote access to information as a means to strengthen political participation by various stakeholder groups in a bottom-up fashion (Weitzman et al., 2006). In this context, access to information is dependent not only on where the information is stored and through what channels it is disseminated but also on how it is derived at in the first place. In addition to the methodological framework, new developments in graphical methods and data visualization can be powerful aides in communicating environmental performance assessments. But frequently, the effectiveness of graphical and visual design tools is diminished due to lack of time (i.e., most time is spent on the development of the performance framework, gathering and analyzing the data, and writing the report), expertise (e.g., graphical designers are not often consulted), and considerations of reproducibility and data dissemination (specialized software may not be available to all users of the assessment). In the case of the EPI, presentation and dissemination were given substantial attention and are considered to have contributed noticeably to its successful dissemination. The EPI consists of a full report and a short policy-focused summary brochure, which is accompanied by a CD-Rom. Both the report and brochure are disseminated in print and as downloadable files through the Internet. To further increase transparency and encourage debate the EPI data are also available by request and for free download. How widely the final product penetrates targeted user segments also depends on the language used. The EPI avoids technical jargon where it does not contribute to better understanding. For global assessments we found that the language of publication also matters. Although the EPI is
