5. Conclusions
This paper addresses some of the challenges that environmental statisticians face at the nexus of environmental statistics and policy, specifically within the context of environmental performance measurement for decisionmaking. Beginning with a lack of a commonly accepted definition of environmental performance, measurement efforts are further hindered by a number of technical and non-technical problems. The Pilot 2006 Environmental Performance Index is used as an example of a global environmental performance measure experiencing many of these problems. The dominant concern today remains the need for more policy-relevant environmental indicators of sufficient quality and coverage. Many developing countries, in particular, find themselves unable to fill the underlying data and capacity gaps without assistance but international environmental indicator initiatives run the risk of favoring global comparability over local utility. The resulting reporting requirements pose an additional burden on national statistical services while the sustained benefits of international measurement initiatives is not always certain. Statisticians tasked with developing environmental indicators increasingly require not only an understanding of the underlying science but also the targeted policy objectives in order to create the best measures from available data. Furthermore, application of sophisticated statistical models without consideration of issues such as transparency, level of existing expertise on the part of the intended users, and aspects of communication and dissemination reduces the potential value of performance assessments. These are nontrivial aspects in that they require public participation and expert consultation and careful balancing of stakeholder preferences. Whenever the measurement objective is not uniformly accepted, the science uncertain, and the need for policy action large, the statistician moves into the area of postnormal statistical science. Being clear about the purposes of the assessment, exact framing of the system to be evaluated, and testing the assessment’s robustness against deviations from the framework’s assumptions is crucial to build credibility and reduce the growing number of seemingly contradicting measures. In addition, societal values, individual worldviews, and political circumstances influence the process of consensus finding and the outcome should hence be reviewed periodically to reflect changing views and conditions.
5.1. The role of environmental statisticians in environmental policy
Environmental statisticians can and should play a dual role in policymaking by supporting and inspiring decision processes. As experts on the collection, analysis, and interpretation of statistical information they generate and analyze the information on which policy choices and regulatory frameworks
are built. They further support decisionmakers by providing scientifically derived testimony on the potential risks, benefits, and other probabilistic events and make projections in time and space that assist policymakers in weighing alternatives, balancing competing objectives, and planning ahead. At the same time, advances in statistical theory and methods facilitate environmental research, reduce uncertainty, and create new paths to knowledge discovery. These contributions by statisticians in turn help inspiring new policy initiatives or adjusting existing ones. Indeed, environmental statistics as a relatively new discipline has made substantial contributions to our understanding of environmental and ecological processes. At the same time the growing complexity and recognition of environmental problems has increased the demands for timely and accurate information at nearly all levels and branches of environmental policy. So what can environmental statisticians do to address the three issues – negative perceptions of environmentalism as a special interest, resistance to the involve quantitative data, analysis, and statisticians in the policy process, and language barriers – described in Section 1.1 and gain the acceptance that other policy-informing disciplines including economics and the health sciences enjoy? Environmental statisticians can probably do little to eliminate the sidelining of environmentalism as a special interest. Developing positive visions, values, and solutions to environmental problems that spark enthusiasm and unite people behind common goals is the challenging task of environmental leaders in government, the non-profit and private sectors as well as academia but statisticians can contribute by providing quantitative benchmarks, scenario analyses, and risk assessments. The argument can thus be made that more environmental statisticians are needed in governmental agencies, large corporations, non-profit research institutions, think tanks, and civil society organizations. Such change cannot come overnight and it has to come primarily from a culture-change within these institutions that recognizes the value of involving quantitative scientists in policymaking. The second issue states that there is a resistance to routinely include quantitative and methodologically rigorous studies in the policymaking process. This can be changed and is changing – in a number of ways. One possibility could be to build-up demand for environmental statisticians through regulation similar to that found in the medical, health, and pharmaceutical sciences. If, for example, approval of activities that can be presumed to have environmental impacts is tied to quantitative impact assessments investigating and estimating such impacts, the role of environmental statistics and statisticians would be greatly enhanced. This procedural practice of tying policy decisions to statistical analysis is already practiced in several countries and sectors11. In addition, applied environmental statisticians could seek to expand the audiences for their work by attending and
