y to rationalize policy debates. In order to remain a relevant science in the future and as the stakes to solve environmental problems are growing higher, environmental statisticians are likely faced with the need to become more involved, to understand diverse policy objectives, and to develop the skills needed for communicating statistical material and developing consensus within the context of multiple, competing interest groups, value-laden science, and considerable uncertainties. This may, inter alia, require a review of the curricula of statistics graduate programs but also an expansion of statistical consulting as well as strengthening the interaction with other environmental policy-relevant disciplines, for example, by organizing meetings between statisticians and policymakers at relevant conventions or other suitable venues.
